In my never-ending quest of middle age, I've been [emulating Mac OS 9](/posts/20260810-newsheep-sheepshaver-mac-os-9-2-apple-silicon-jit/) and [getting Johnny Castaway running in a browser](/posts/20261019-johnny-castaway-browser/). Playing board games with my kids has me revisiting more of that stuff too. I have a rant brewing about the quality of *The Game of Life* these days, but that can wait.

The '90s seem to be back in style, though much of what I notice is the grungier side: distressed type and rough textures. I keep going back to the chaotic graphics on Japanese Sonic covers. I always thought those were impressive. Enormous lettering, bright colours, squiggles and zigzag edges, all fitted around a character who already takes up most of the cover.

I wanted some of that personality here. I'd only recently redesigned the site, and the result felt too clinical. The analytical style suited my work, but it was a bit sterile for my own blog.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - Japanese Sonic covers supplied the yellow disc, bold colour fields, compressed type and patterned edges.
> - A TurboGrafx-16 ad suggested a cut-paper treatment for selected images.
> - The homepage gets the expressive composition; the articles keep a clear reading column and upright text.

## From a datasheet to a cover

The previous design had graph paper, numbered sections, small labels and rules around most things. By the time I'd added them all, the homepage looked like a technical document. I wanted to loosen it up.

I tried removing the frames. That helped, but left an awkward step where the headline started at the page edge and the biography jumped into another column. The alignment made sense in the stylesheet and looked awkward on the page.

The cartridge covers gave me something to work with: big type, a few strong colours and room for things to overlap. Having worked in newspaper graphics and print production, I also liked thinking of the homepage as a cover with the writing behind it.

