Japanese Mega Drive packaging, reproduced here as design references. Artwork and branding © SEGA. See the [VGDensetsu archive for the covers and its illustration and packaging credits](https://vgdensetsu.net/megadrivegallerys3/); individual design credits have not been independently verified here.

I borrowed the yellow disc for the headline, with the first line in black and the second in cobalt. The short description of what I do sits on a black ribbon: “Platforms. Content. People. Connected by design.” A red diagonal sits behind the blue project card.

The squiggles, triangles and serrated edges were fun to work with. Some turned up around code blocks and quotations too, with the decoration kept out of the text itself.

Looking into those references also led to [Sottsass’s *Casablanca* and *Carlton* drawings at the V&A](https://www.vam.ac.uk/info/collection-selection-boxes-postmodern-design) and [April Greiman’s work in *Eye on Design*](https://eyeondesign.aiga.org/april-greiman-is-still-ahead-of-the-curve/). I was looking at the angles, oversized lettering and how much the type could do on its own. The Sonic covers were still the main reference.

I used new SVG marks for the site. The character illustrations and SEGA branding appear only in the reference images.

## A second reference: fragments on paper

I had a TurboGrafx-16 growing up and loved it. [This ad from *Electronic Gaming Monthly*](https://archives.tg-16.com/EGM_1989_07.htm) caught my eye too: a face and game screenshots cut into odd shapes, with bits of red and yellow overlapping them. There is a lot happening, but also a surprising amount of empty paper.

